#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"id2\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mode-based indexing {#mode-based-indexing}"
msgstr "Mode-based na pag-index {#mode-based-indexing}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "All operator representations refer to the indices that their terms act upon as **modes**. A mode is simply an index identifying a fermionic degree of freedom in your system. The mapping from physical degrees of freedom (such as spatial orbitals, spin states, or other quantum numbers) to mode indices is left to the user, enabling maximum flexibility.\n"
msgstr "Tinatawag na **mode** ng lahat ng representasyon ng operator ang mga index na ginagalawan ng kanilang mga term. Ang mode ay isang index lang na tumutukoy sa isang fermionic degree of freedom sa sistema mo. Ikaw na ang bahala sa pag-map ng mga pisikal na degree of freedom (tulad ng spatial orbital, spin state, o iba pang quantum number) papunta sa mga mode index, kaya sobrang flexible nito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This abstraction is also present in the [`qiskit_fermions.circuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit#module-qiskit_fermions.circuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit\") module, where the [`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") operates on a register of fermionic modes. In both the operator and circuit representations, modes provide a consistent, algebra-independent way to specify which degrees of freedom participate in a given operation.\n"
msgstr "Nandito rin ang abstraksiyong ito sa module na [`qiskit_fermions.circuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit#module-qiskit_fermions.circuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit\"), kung saan ang [`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") ay gumagana sa isang register ng mga fermionic mode. Sa parehong representasyon ng operator at circuit, nagbibigay ang mga mode ng pare-parehong paraan, na hindi nakadepende sa algebra, para tukuyin kung aling mga degree of freedom ang kasali sa isang operasyon.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  **Current implementations use spinless modes**: All operator representations currently provided by this module treat modes as spinless fermionic degrees of freedom. This means if your system has both spin-up and spin-down electrons or fermions, you must explicitly map them to distinct modes (for example, modes 0-3 for spin-up of four spatial orbitals, modes 4-7 for spin-down, or any other convention you choose).\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  **Spinless na mode ang gamit ng mga kasalukuyang implementasyon**: Itinuturing ng lahat ng representasyon ng operator na ibinibigay ngayon ng module na ito ang mga mode bilang spinless na fermionic degree of freedom. Ibig sabihin, kung may spin-up at spin-down na electron o fermion ang sistema mo, kailangan mo silang tahasang i-map sa magkakaibang mode (halimbawa, mode 0-3 para sa spin-up ng apat na spatial orbital, mode 4-7 para sa spin-down, o anumang ibang convention na piliin mo).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  This design keeps the core representations simple and general while avoiding imposing a specific spin-ordering convention. Utility modules like [`qiskit_fermions.operators.library`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library#module-qiskit_fermions.operators.library \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library\") provide convenience functions (for example, [`FCIDump.from_file()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library-fci-dump#qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file\")) that handle such mappings for you when loading electronic structure data.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"  Pinananatili ng disenyong ito na simple at pangkalahatan ang mga pangunahing representasyon habang iniiwasang magpataw ng partikular na convention sa pag-order ng spin. Ang mga utility module tulad ng [`qiskit_fermions.operators.library`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library#module-qiskit_fermions.operators.library \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library\") ay nagbibigay ng mga convenience function (halimbawa, [`FCIDump.from_file()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library-fci-dump#qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file\")) na nag-aasikaso ng mga ganitong mapping para sa iyo kapag naglo-load ng electronic structure data.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  As the package evolves, spinful operator representations might be added that natively support spin degrees of freedom in their data model. These will be clearly distinguished from the current spinless implementations and will coexist with them in the module.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Habang umuunlad ang package, maaaring madagdagan ng mga spinful na representasyon ng operator na natively sumusuporta sa mga spin degree of freedom sa kanilang data model. Malinaw silang ihihiwalay sa mga kasalukuyang spinless na implementasyon at magkakasama sila sa module.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"term-grouping\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Term grouping and commutation relationships {#term-grouping-and-commutation-relationships}"
msgstr "Pag-grupo ng mga term at mga ugnayan ng commutation {#term-grouping-and-commutation-relationships}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Like the coefficients and mode indices, operators can optionally store a **groups array** that associates each term with a group index. By integrating grouping directly into the operator representation as part of the sparse data structure, grouping information naturally travels with the operator through transformations. This enables systematic structure usage, whether from physical properties, algebraic relationships, or problem-specific symmetries. The structured information can then be used in downstream operations like circuit synthesis and decomposition by using methods like [`OperatorTrait.split_out_groups()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups\").\n"
msgstr "Tulad ng mga coefficient at mode index, puwedeng mag-imbak ang mga operator ng **groups array** bilang opsyon, na iniuugnay ang bawat term sa isang group index. Dahil nakasama na ang pag-grupo sa representasyon ng operator bilang bahagi ng sparse na estruktura ng data, natural na sumasama ang impormasyon ng pag-grupo sa operator sa bawat transformation. Nagbibigay-daan ito sa sistematikong paggamit ng estruktura, galing man sa mga pisikal na katangian, algebraic na ugnayan, o simetriya na partikular sa problema. Magagamit ang estrukturang impormasyong ito sa mga susunod na operasyon tulad ng circuit synthesis at decomposition gamit ang mga paraan tulad ng [`OperatorTrait.split_out_groups()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups\").\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For detailed guidance on how to group operator terms in your workflows, see the [grouping](grouping#grouping-explanation) guide.\n"
msgstr "Para sa detalyadong gabay kung paano i-grupo ang mga term ng operator sa mga workflow mo, tingnan ang gabay sa [pag-grupo](grouping#grouping-explanation).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import MajoranaOperator, gamma\n"
"    >>> op = MajoranaOperator.from_dict({\n"
"    ...     (gamma(0, False),): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (gamma(1, False),): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (gamma(2, False), gamma(3, False)): 1.0\n"
"    ... })\n"
"    >>> # Assign group indices to terms\n"
"    >>> op.groups = [0, 0, 1]\n"
"    >>> # Partition operator by groups\n"
"    >>> grouped_ops = op.split_out_groups()\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Create operator with 3 terms\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[3] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 2, 3};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[4] = {0, 1, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *op = qf_maj_op_new(3, 4, coeffs, modes, boundaries);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Create operator with 3 terms\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[3] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 2, 3};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[4] = {0, 1, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *op = qf_maj_op_new(3, 4, coeffs, modes, boundaries);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Assign grouping information\n"
"    uint32_t groups[3] = {0, 0, 1};\n"
"    qf_maj_op_set_groups(op, groups, 3);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Assign grouping information\n"
"    uint32_t groups[3] = {0, 0, 1};\n"
"    qf_maj_op_set_groups(op, groups, 3);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Partition operator by groups\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *grouped_ops[2];\n"
"    qf_maj_op_split_out_groups(op, NULL, 0, grouped_ops);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Partition operator by groups\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *grouped_ops[2];\n"
"    qf_maj_op_split_out_groups(op, NULL, 0, grouped_ops);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"id3\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Arithmetic and mathematical operations {#arithmetic-and-mathematical-operations}"
msgstr "Mga operasyong aritmetika at matematikal {#arithmetic-and-mathematical-operations}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "All operators implement the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol, which provides a unified set of operations across different operator types. This ensures that code written for one operator representation works uniformly with others. See [`qiskit_fermions.protocols`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/protocols#module-qiskit_fermions.protocols \"qiskit_fermions.protocols\") for how this protocol relates to the others this package defines.\n"
msgstr "Ipinapatupad ng lahat ng operator ang protocol na [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\"), na nagbibigay ng pinag-isang hanay ng mga operasyon sa iba't ibang uri ng operator. Tinitiyak nito na ang code na isinulat para sa isang representasyon ng operator ay gumagana rin nang pareho sa iba. Tingnan ang [`qiskit_fermions.protocols`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/protocols#module-qiskit_fermions.protocols \"qiskit_fermions.protocols\") para malaman kung paano nauugnay ang protocol na ito sa iba pang tinutukoy ng package na ito.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The protocol includes arithmetic operations (such as addition, multiplication, and composition), structural operations (term iteration, mode support analysis, relabeling), mathematical functions (normal ordering, simplification, equivalence checking), and more. See the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") documentation for a complete reference of all available operations.\n"
msgstr "Kasama sa protocol ang mga operasyong aritmetika (tulad ng addition, multiplication, at composition), mga estrukturang operasyon (pag-iterate ng term, pagsusuri ng mode support, relabeling), mga matematikal na function (normal ordering, simplification, pagsusuri ng equivalence), at iba pa. Tingnan ang documentation ng [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") para sa kumpletong reference ng lahat ng available na operasyon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    >>> op = FermionOperator.from_dict({\n"
"    ...     (cre(0), ann(1)): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (cre(1), ann(0)): 1.0\n"
"    ... })\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    >>> print(f\"Operator is Hermitian: {op.is_hermitian(atol=1e-10)}\")\n"
"    Operator is Hermitian: True\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[2] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions[4] = {true, false, true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 1, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[3] = {0, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op = qf_ferm_op_new(2, 4, coeffs, actions, modes, boundaries);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[2] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions[4] = {true, false, true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 1, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[3] = {0, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op = qf_ferm_op_new(2, 4, coeffs, actions, modes, boundaries);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    bool is_hermitian = qf_ferm_op_is_hermitian(op, 1e-10);\n"
"    printf(\"Operator is Hermitian: %s\\n\", is_hermitian ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    bool is_hermitian = qf_ferm_op_is_hermitian(op, 1e-10);\n"
"    printf(\"Operator is Hermitian: %s\\n\", is_hermitian ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Clean up\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Clean up\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<DoqAdmonition type=\"note\" title=\"Note\">\n"
msgstr "<DoqAdmonition type=\"note\" title=\"Tandaan\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") verifies that $H - H^\\dagger = 0$ by reducing that difference to a normal form and comparing it against [`zero()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero\"). Spelled out in terms of the other protocol operations, it performs the following:\n"
msgstr "  Bine-verify ng [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") na $H - H^\\dagger = 0$ sa pamamagitan ng pagpapababa ng pagkakaibang iyon sa normal form at paghahambing nito sa [`zero()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero\"). Kung isusulat gamit ang iba pang operasyon ng protocol, ito ang ginagawa nito:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  The strength of this check depends on the operator type, because it depends on how completely that type’s normal form contracts adjacent generators. A `True` result is always reliable, but for some types a `False` result is conservative: see [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") for details.\n"
msgstr "  Nakadepende sa uri ng operator ang lakas ng pagsusuring ito, dahil nakasalalay ito sa kung gaano kakumpleto ang pag-contract ng normal form ng uring iyon sa magkakatabing generator. Laging maaasahan ang resultang `True`, pero sa ilang uri, konserbatibo ang resultang `False`: tingnan ang [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") para sa mga detalye.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "</DoqAdmonition>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  The example uses `atol=1e-10`. The `atol` (absolute tolerance) parameter specifies a threshold. Coefficients with magnitude smaller than `atol` are treated as zero and discarded. This is essential for numerical stability when comparing operators, since floating-point arithmetic can introduce small rounding errors that would otherwise prevent equivalent operators from being recognized as such.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Gumagamit ang halimbawa ng `atol=1e-10`. Ang parameter na `atol` (absolute tolerance) ay nagtatakda ng threshold. Ang mga coefficient na mas maliit ang magnitude kaysa sa `atol` ay itinuturing na zero at itinatapon. Mahalaga ito para sa numerical stability sa paghahambing ng mga operator, dahil ang floating-point arithmetic ay maaaring magdulot ng maliliit na rounding error na hahadlang sa pagkilala sa magkakapantay na operator bilang magkapantay.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  While the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol provides a common interface, individual operator implementations might offer additional convenience methods not part of the protocol. For example, [`FermionOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-fermion-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator\") and [`MajoranaOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-majorana-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator\") provide a `max_rank()` method that reports the length of their longest term. Always consult the API documentation for your operator type to discover all available functionality.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Bagama't nagbibigay ang protocol na [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") ng karaniwang interface, maaaring mag-alok ang bawat implementasyon ng operator ng mga karagdagang convenience method na hindi bahagi ng protocol. Halimbawa, ang [`FermionOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-fermion-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator\") at [`MajoranaOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-majorana-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator\") ay nagbibigay ng method na `max_rank()` na nag-uulat ng haba ng pinakamahabang term nila. Lagi mong basahin ang API documentation ng uri ng operator mo para malaman ang lahat ng available na functionality.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"operator-term-ordering\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Operator term ordering and normal forms {#operator-term-ordering-and-normal-forms}"
msgstr "Pag-order ng term ng operator at mga normal form {#operator-term-ordering-and-normal-forms}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A fundamental challenge in quantum operator algebra is that mathematically equivalent operators can be represented in many different ways, each with different implications for quantum algorithms. The same operator can be written in algebraically equivalent forms (for example, $a^\\dagger b$ can be expressed as $ba^\\dagger + [a^\\dagger,b]$), yet these representations lead to different behavior in circuit synthesis, simplification, and numerical algorithms.\n"
msgstr "Isang pangunahing hamon sa quantum operator algebra ay ang magkakapantay na operator sa matematika ay maaaring katawanin sa maraming magkakaibang paraan, na bawat isa ay may iba't ibang implikasyon para sa mga quantum algorithm. Maisusulat ang iisang operator sa mga algebraically equivalent na anyo (halimbawa, ang $a^\\dagger b$ ay maaaring ipahayag bilang $ba^\\dagger + [a^\\dagger,b]$), pero ang mga representasyong ito ay humahantong sa magkakaibang behavior sa circuit synthesis, simplification, at mga numerical algorithm.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The operator representations support **normal ordering** operations that transform operators into algebra-specific canonical forms using commutation relations. This enables reliable comparisons (two equivalent operators have identical normal-ordered forms), reveals simplifications (commutation relations cause terms to cancel or combine), and supports algorithms that require specific operator forms for correctness and efficiency.\n"
msgstr "Sinusuportahan ng mga representasyon ng operator ang mga operasyong **normal ordering** na nagta-transform ng mga operator sa mga canonical form na partikular sa algebra gamit ang mga commutation relation. Nagbibigay-daan ito sa maaasahang paghahambing (ang dalawang magkapantay na operator ay may magkaparehong normal-ordered na anyo), nagbubunyag ng mga simplification (ang mga commutation relation ay nagiging dahilan ng pagkansela o pagsasama ng mga term), at sumusuporta sa mga algorithm na nangangailangan ng partikular na anyo ng operator para sa tamang resulta at kahusayan.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Different operator representations might implement normal ordering based on different commutation relations appropriate to their algebra. For example, fermionic normal ordering uses anticommutation relations ($\\{c_i, c_j^\\dagger\\} = \\delta_{ij}$), while Majorana normal ordering uses different algebra conventions ($\\{\\gamma_i, \\gamma_j\\} = 2\\delta_{ij}$). Always consult the documentation for your operator type to understand how normal ordering is implemented.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Maaaring ipatupad ng magkakaibang representasyon ng operator ang normal ordering batay sa iba't ibang commutation relation na angkop sa kanilang algebra. Halimbawa, gumagamit ang fermionic normal ordering ng mga anticommutation relation ($\\{c_i, c_j^\\dagger\\} = \\delta_{ij}$), habang ang Majorana normal ordering ay gumagamit ng ibang convention ng algebra ($\\{\\gamma_i, \\gamma_j\\} = 2\\delta_{ij}$). Lagi mong basahin ang documentation ng uri ng operator mo para maintindihan kung paano ipinatutupad ang normal ordering.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Two different representations of the same operator\n"
"    >>> op1 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(ann(0), cre(0)): 1.0})\n"
"    >>> op2 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(): 1.0, (cre(0), ann(0)): -1.0})\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Direct comparison fails due to different forms\n"
"    >>> op1.equiv(op2, atol=1e-10)\n"
"    False\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Normal-order both and compare again\n"
"    >>> op1_normal = op1.normal_ordered()\n"
"    >>> op2_normal = op2.normal_ordered()\n"
"    >>> op1_normal.equiv(op2_normal, atol=1e-10)\n"
"    True\n"
"    ```\n"
